      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I don't think the two requests are mutually exclusive! It very much depends on the type of world you're in.\n**Avatar range limits** would lessen GPU load (by rendering fewer players). That's good for DJ events, or worlds that are like big boxes: I can see everyone at once, there's nowhere to hide.\nAnd **better culling** would decrease CPU load (no collision, audio, etc). That's good for any world with rooms, walls and different areas. Most players are already hidden by Unity's occlusion culling system, but my CPU is still running a lot of code for those players.\n**Both** would help! 😁\nPersonally, I kind of like being able to see 80 people at once. If a world creator did not want me to do that, they could have designed their world differently, or lowered the max player cap. I'd also want to avoid situations where someone waves at me, but I had them hidden!\n**Edit**: VRChat's most recent blog post announced that they'll let us do both. Nice.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
